    "summary": "Stellar Let It Grow & epic He's Gone > Truckin' > The Other One",
    "blurb": "The show shines with a soaring Let It Grow backed by the new horn section and an unforgettable He's Gone → Truckin' → The Other One → Wharf Rat marathon. Jerry’s tone is buttery, Phil lays down rock‑solid bass, and Keith adds lively keys. Highlights include Black Peter, Jack Straw and a sweet Row Jimmy.",
    "review": "September 21, 1973 at the Spectrum captured the Dead in top form. The first set rolls through classic early‑’70 material – Big Railroad Blues, Sugaree and a tight Jack Straw – before a standout Black Peter that feels freshly inspired. The second half is where the night truly ignites: Let It Grow swells with Doug Sahm’s horn section, then the band launches into an unbroken jam that weaves He's Gone into Truckin', morphs into The Other One and erupts into Wharf Rat, all propelled by Bill’s crisp drumming and Keith’s lively organ fills. Row Jimmy showcases Garcia’s sweet soloing while Casey Jones closes with the kind of joyous drive only a Spectrum crowd could coax. Across multiple recordings the performance is consistently praised for its energy, superb musicianship and the added color of the horns, making this one of the era’s must‑hear shows.",
